@page "/index.html"
@inherits IndexPage

<PageTitle>BrowserCommander Setup</PageTitle>

<section class="welcome-shell">
    <article class="welcome-card">
        <p class="eyebrow">BrowserCommander</p>
        <h1>Quick start</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            The extension is only one part of the setup. You also need the local server,
            an MCP CLI or client, and a matching local server address in the extension options.
        </p>

        <ol class="steps">
            <li>
                <strong>Install and start the local program</strong>
                <p>You need the local MCP server. For development, you can start it like this:</p>
                <pre><code>dotnet run --project BrowserCommanderServer/BrowserCommanderServer.csproj --launch-profile http</code></pre>
                <p class="note">The portable bundle and the default MCP endpoint use <code>http://localhost:5082/mcp</code>.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>Check the extension settings</strong>
                <p>Open the extension <code>Options</code> page and make sure the server address matches your local install.</p>
                <p class="note">This alpha build only allows loopback addresses such as <code>http://localhost:5082</code>.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>Configure your MCP CLI or client</strong>
                <p>If your client uses stdio, start it through the bridge process:</p>
                <pre><code>command: C:\Tools\BrowserCommander\BrowserCommander.McpStdioBridge.exe
cwd: C:\Tools\BrowserCommander</code></pre>
                <p class="note">If your client supports HTTP MCP directly, point it to <code>http://localhost:5082/mcp</code>.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <strong>Authorize the current tab</strong>
                <p>Open the extension popup and click <code>Authorize Current Tab</code>.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </article>
</section>
